Project 8: Quality Assurance & Food Safety – Hygiene and Preventive Control

Project Overview

This project demonstrates my understanding of Quality Assurance, food safety, personal hygiene, monitoring, corrective actions, documentation, and preventive measures in a food production environment.

Scenario

During a routine inspection of a food production area, I observed a worker handling food without properly washing their hands.

Objective

To identify the food-safety risk, take immediate corrective action, investigate the possible cause, and establish preventive measures to reduce the risk of food contamination.

Initial Observation

A worker was observed handling food without following the required handwashing procedure.

Immediate Action

I would stop the unsafe activity and inform the worker that proper hand hygiene is required before handling food. I would ensure that the worker properly washes their hands before continuing food-handling activities.

Risk Identified

Poor hand hygiene can transfer microorganisms from a person’s hands to food, equipment, or other surfaces. This can result in food contamination and potentially contribute to foodborne illness.

Investigation

I would investigate:

* Whether the worker had received proper food-hygiene training.
* Whether handwashing facilities were available.
* Whether soap and clean water were available.
* Whether hygiene procedures were clearly communicated.
* Whether other workers were following the required hygiene practices.

Possible Cause

Possible causes could include inadequate training, poor supervision, lack of awareness, or inadequate access to handwashing facilities.

The exact cause should be investigated before deciding on the appropriate corrective action.

Corrective Action

I would ensure that the worker follows the correct handwashing procedure before returning to food handling.

If food may have been contaminated, it would be assessed and handled according to the company’s food-safety procedures.

Preventive Action

To prevent similar incidents:

* Provide regular food-hygiene training.
* Display clear handwashing instructions.
* Monitor personal hygiene practices regularly.
* Ensure adequate soap, clean water, and handwashing facilities.
* Properly supervise food-handling activities.
* Conduct regular food-safety inspections.

Documentation

The incident should be documented, including:

* The hygiene problem observed.
* Date, time, and location.
* Corrective action taken.
* Any affected food or production area.
* Possible cause of the problem.
* Preventive measures recommended.
* Follow-up actions.

Result

The hygiene issue was identified and corrective action was taken immediately.

The worker was instructed to follow the proper handwashing procedure before continuing food-handling activities. The production area was monitored to ensure that appropriate hygiene practices were being followed.

The incident was documented, and preventive measures such as regular hygiene training, supervision, and routine inspections were recommended.

Skills Demonstrated

* Quality Assurance
* Food safety
* Personal hygiene monitoring
* Hazard identification
* Corrective and preventive action (CAPA)
* Food contamination prevention
* Inspection and monitoring
* Documentation and record keeping
* Staff communication
* Problem-solving
* Safety awareness

Conclusion

Good food safety depends on preventing contamination throughout the production process. Proper personal hygiene, regular monitoring, employee training, effective corrective actions, and accurate documentation help protect food quality and consumer safety.

This project demonstrates my understanding of how Quality Assurance principles can be applied to identify and prevent food-safety risks in a food production environment.